  "hints": [
    "Counting subarrays with exactly k distinct values head-on forces a decision that has no clean rule: when the window holds k + 1 distinct values, you cannot tell whether to move the left edge or to keep extending.",
    "Count something with an easy rule instead: subarrays with at most k distinct values. For a fixed right end, the valid starts form one contiguous block, from the shortest valid window's left edge through right.",
    "Exactly k is at most k minus at most k-1, and each of those is a single two-pointer pass with a frequency table. Remember that a window with span right - left + 1 admits exactly right - left + 1 valid starts."
  ],
  "editorial": {
    "approach": "The brute force fixes a start index, then extends the end index one step at a time while maintaining a frequency map and a running distinct count. Each extension increments the counter when a value appears for the first time in the window; when the distinct count equals k the subarray is counted, and as soon as it exceeds k the inner loop breaks, because extending further can only add more distinct values. The optimal avoids the direct 'exactly k' window, where no single shrink rule is correct, and instead counts the complementary quantity: exactly(k) = atMost(k) - atMost(k-1). atMost(L) counts subarrays with at most L distinct values using one sliding window: for each right end, advance left while the window holds more than L distinct values; the window is then the shortest valid one ending at right, and every start between left and right yields a valid subarray, so right - left + 1 subarrays are added.",
    "why_optimal": "Each atMost pass runs in O(n): right advances n times and left advances at most n times in total, with O(1) work per step thanks to a frequency array indexed by value. Two passes make the whole algorithm linear, which is the best possible because the answer itself can be quadratic (n = 200 with one repeated value and k = 1 gives 20100) and the input still has to be read once. The value bound nums[i] <= 50 lets the frequency table be a fixed array of 51 counters instead of a hash map, which is what keeps the constant small. Memory is O(n) in the worst case for the map-based version, or O(max value) with the array version.",
    "pitfalls": "The k = 1 case routes through atMost(0), which must return 0 immediately instead of entering the window loop with a limit of zero. When shrinking the window, decrement the outgoing value's frequency and only reduce the distinct count when it reaches exactly 0; deleting the key instead of storing 0, or testing the wrong comparison, makes the distinct counter drift upwards and the window never recovers. Each valid window contributes right - left + 1 subarrays, not 1, because every start in the window's span is itself a valid subarray. Breaking out of the brute-force inner loop too late (counting subarrays with more than k distinct values) inflates the answer, and starting the brute-force extension from an empty map per i rather than reusing state is a common source of wrong repeated values."
  },
